  "worked_solution": "To find when the number of people is at a minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2t-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n2t-4=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=2\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=2.$ To find out how many people are in the festival when $t=2,$ we figure out $T(2)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(2) & =10+{\\int }_{0}^{2}2x-4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10+{[{x}^{2}-4x]|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =10+[({2}^{2}-4(2))-(0)] \\\\  & =10-4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $6\\,000<10\\,000$, we conclude that the minimum number of people is $6\\,000$, and this will happen after $2$ hours.",
